WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.550
Three more kinds of fire, each with its own trick.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.400
Electrical.

3
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.375
Kill the power first, at the battery switch or the breaker.

4
00:00:09.625 --> 00:00:13.000
Then use carbon dioxide or dry chemical.

5
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.275
With the power off, it’s often just a small Class A fire.

6
00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:19.225
Galley grease.

7
00:00:19.475 --> 00:00:23.650
Smother it with a lid or a fire blanket, and turn off the heat.

8
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.625
Never water.

9
00:00:25.875 --> 00:00:30.425
Water turns to steam and throws burning grease across the cabin.

10
00:00:30.675 --> 00:00:32.175
Propane.

11
00:00:32.425 --> 00:00:34.475
Close the valve at the tank.

12
00:00:34.725 --> 00:00:41.375
And remember that propane is heavier than air, so a leak sinks into the bilge just like gasoline vapor.

13
00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:45.650
If you smell it, ventilate before you touch a single switch.

14
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.375
And keep a fire blanket handy.

15
00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:51.975
It’s the fastest way to put out burning clothing, too.

16
00:00:52.975 --> 00:00:54.900
That’s lesson three.

17
00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.025
Heat, fuel, oxygen, chain reaction.

18
00:00:58.275 --> 00:01:01.250
A for ash, B for boil, C for current.

19
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.625
PASS, aimed at the base.

20
00:01:03.875 --> 00:01:07.400
And never open the engine hatch for fifteen minutes.

21
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.225
Next time: how to keep the fire from starting at all.
